Hi Haiying,

Here's our answers to your questions/comments:

> KIP-1150 is aiming for both diskless and leaderless. I am not sure why=
 it is a good idea trying to solve both in one KIP.  This significantly =
increase the scope of this project with the complexity of solving many r=
elated issues (transaction, queue support, data locality) in the same pr=
oject.

Leaderless is essentially necessary to eliminate the inter-AZ network co=
sts completely, including the producer<->broker part, which was the main=
 motivation behind this project. See also the following paragraph for th=
e clarification on KIP-1123.

> I think with KIP-392 (Fetch from follower) and KIP-1123 (Rack aware pa=
rtitioning for Kafka Producer), both producer and consumer can read/writ=
e to a broker other than the original leader broker. The leader broker i=
s no longer the blocker component in the read/write paths. With the KIP-=
405 tiered storage, the leader state is also much smaller than before.

I=E2=80=99d like to clarify on KIP-1123: unfortunately, it doesn=E2=80=99=
t allow writing to any broker. Classic partitions still have leaders whi=
ch are the only point where write could be done. KIP-1123 allows the pro=
ducer to pick a partition in the rack-aware manner, but this comes with =
obvious limitations: 1) it=E2=80=99s suitable only for use cases where t=
he partition doesn=E2=80=99t matter and could be picked arbitrarily; 2) =
there must be enough partitions (i.e. single partition topics are not co=
vered, for example).

> By removing the leader broker in the revised KIP-1150 design, you woul=
d need to move many logic (e.g. offset assignment, transaction control) =
which was originally in the leader to the batch coordinator, this adds t=
o the latency and complicated the logic (those logic are easier to imple=
ment in a single leader broker).=20
>=20
> In the current Kafka design, the control is distributed in many leader=
 brokers with no hotspots for the whole cluster. But KIP-1150 is moving =
the distributed control into a central component: batch coordinator whic=
h adds a hotspot/bottleneck for the cluster;  And this batch coordinator=
 needs to be involved in transaction control as well which adds more bot=
tleneck/latency in a critical control flow.

We expect that there will be substantial reuse of logic between the lead=
er and batch coordinator. The code will need to be refactored to permit =
decoupling data and metadata, but as that is the core added value of our=
 proposal and enables leaderless data handling, it appears to be worth t=
he investment.

The Batch Coordinator, while being a single component in the design, nee=
d not be a single node. It will be somewhat similar to the existing Grou=
p, Share Group, and Transaction coordinators and utilize partitioning to=
 scale horizontally. With proper sizing, we expect that it will be no mo=
re of a hotspot than any classic user partition.

Best,
Ivan

