Hi Kevin, thanks for reading and responding to the KIP!


>  What is the motivation for lowering this default limit from 8MiB to 1MiB=
?


I think that a 1MiB default is more appropriate for most users. The new
configuration slows things down (slightly) in a way which does not affect
availability but is *correct* in more cases. As such I think it's more
appropriate as a default. Keeping the 8MiB value means users are more
likely to run into the liveness problem and take corrective action.
Debugging it requires knowledge of the KRaft protocol. Further, it is
already possible to mitigate the problem by increasing the leader election
timeout. The intent of setting the default to 1MiB is to make the scenario
less likely to occur given that we probably don't loose "too much" in terms
of performance.

Metadata snapshots are not typically larger than 5 MiB. The largest I have
seen was ~=3D 40 MiB. Further, the slow down would be a result of the
underlying TCP connection being as utilized as much as before. The
connection cannot send up to 8MiB of data inflight at any given time.
Changing the configuration from 8 -> 1 does not mean that an operation
which used to take 1 second will now take 8 seconds. It's possible that
multiple 1MiB requests can be sent per second, especially on a higher
performance network. The same underlying TCP connection is being used
regardless which means handshakes and transmission windows don't need to be
recomputed each time a request is sent.

Best,
Jonah


